WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:36.417
Receive the public request. The browser talks to one public address. The reverse proxy represents backend servers rather than acting as the browser own outbound proxy.

2
00:00:36.417 --> 00:01:11.917
Apply forwarding rules. Host and path rules select a backend. Request size, deadlines, TLS termination and supported protocols must fit the actual workload.

3
00:01:11.917 --> 00:01:52.000
Forward the request. API traffic reaches the API backend and asset traffic can reach another backend. A reverse proxy can also perform load-balancing duties.

4
00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:21.333
Preserve trusted context. Forward useful request context, but do not accept forged identity or client-IP headers from arbitrary clients as trusted facts.

5
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:55.500
Handle streaming deliberately. Buffering and idle/read timeouts can change what a browser sees. Test streams and long-lived connections end to end instead of assuming default behavior fits.

6
00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:39.250
Protect the proxy tier. The public entry point needs its own capacity, redundancy and monitoring. Backend replicas do not help if the only proxy is unavailable.
